    <main class="lesson-content">
        <article class="lesson-article">
            <header class="lesson-header"><span class="lesson-number">Lesson 15</span><h1>Avoiding Common Pitfalls</h1><div class="lesson-meta"><span class="lesson-duration"><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><polyline points="12 6 12 12 16 14"/></svg>30 min</span><span class="lesson-level intermediate">Intermediate</span></div></header>
            <div class="lesson-body">
                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>❌ Pitfall 1: async void</h2>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// ❌ BAD - exceptions are lost, can't be awaited
async void DoWork()
{
    await Task.Delay(100);
    throw new Exception("Lost forever!");
}

// ✅ GOOD - return Task
async Task DoWorkAsync()
{
    await Task.Delay(100);
}

// Exception: Event handlers CAN use async void
button.Click += async (s, e) =&gt; await HandleClickAsync();</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>❌ Pitfall 2: Blocking on Async (Deadlock!)</h2>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// ❌ DEADLOCK in UI/ASP.NET apps!
public string GetData()
{
    return GetDataAsync().Result;  // Blocks!
}

// ❌ Also deadlock
public string GetData()
{
    return GetDataAsync().GetAwaiter().GetResult();
}

// ✅ GOOD - async all the way
public async Task&lt;string&gt; GetDataAsync()
{
    return await httpClient.GetStringAsync(url);
}</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>❌ Pitfall 3: Not Using ConfigureAwait</h2>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// In library code, always use ConfigureAwait(false)
public async Task&lt;Data&gt; LibraryMethodAsync()
{
    // Don't capture sync context
    var result = await httpClient.GetAsync(url).ConfigureAwait(false);
    return await result.Content.ReadFromJsonAsync&lt;Data&gt;().ConfigureAwait(false);
}</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>❌ Pitfall 4: Not Passing CancellationToken</h2>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// ❌ BAD - ignores cancellation
public async Task ProcessAsync()
{
    await httpClient.GetStringAsync(url);  // Can't cancel!
}

// ✅ GOOD - accept and pass token
public async Task ProcessAsync(CancellationToken ct = default)
{
    await httpClient.GetStringAsync(url, ct);
}</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>❌ Pitfall 5: Using Task.Run for I/O</h2>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// ❌ BAD - wastes a thread pool thread
var data = await Task.Run(() =&gt; httpClient.GetStringAsync(url));

// ✅ GOOD - just await directly
var data = await httpClient.GetStringAsync(url);</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Key Takeaways</h2>
                    <div class="takeaways">
                        <LessonTakeaway>Never use async void (except event handlers).</LessonTakeaway>
                        <LessonTakeaway>Never block on async with .Result or .Wait().</LessonTakeaway>
                        <LessonTakeaway>Use ConfigureAwait(false) in library code.</LessonTakeaway>
                        <LessonTakeaway>Always pass CancellationToken through call chains.</LessonTakeaway>
                    </div>
                </section>
            </div>
